<h1> U.S. Kids Golf: A Beginner’s Guide to Tournament Play </h1>
<h2> A First Tournament Handbook from a Player’s Perspective </h2>
<h2> By Brian Li, President of the Boston Eaglets Club </h2>
<h3> April 1, 2026 </h3>
 
<p>
When I played my first U.S. Kids Golf tournament, I didn’t fully understand all the rules, expectations, or even what really mattered during a round. Like many beginners, my parents and I learned through experience sometimes the hard way.
This guide is meant to make that first experience easier for you and your family. It brings together key lessons about tournament play, from pacing and rules to how players, caddies, and spectators should work together.


Most importantly, your first tournament is not about playing perfectly. It’s about learning, gaining
confidence, and enjoying the game. Every round will help you grow.


Good luck, and enjoy the journey ! 
<br>
1. Full Tournament Workflow
Arrive 45–60 minutes early with full equipment: clubs, at least 6 balls, tees, ball marker, glove, and water.
Check-in: receive scorecard, confirm tee time and group, review local rules.
Warm-up: putting first (most important), then chipping, then full swing. Avoid over-practice.
Be at the tee 10 minutes early, meet your group, and listen to the starter.


During play:
- Play the ball as it lies
- Farthest from the hole plays first
- Water hazard: +1 penalty (drop or replay)
- OB: +1 penalty + replay from previous spot
- Drop from knee height
- Ball search limit: 3 minutes
- No touching sand before bunker shot
- Repair ball marks and respect putting lines


After each hole: confirm score with group and record immediately.
After round: double-check all scores, sign card, and submit. Errors can lead to disqualification.
💡 Boston Eaglets Tip: Pace of play and score confirmation are the two most common mistakes—stay ahead of both.
<br>
2. Caddie Rules
Before each shot:
- Stand to the side/back of the player
- Give short, simple advice
- Do NOT stand directly behind to help alignment

During the shot:
- Stay completely silent
- Do not move

After the shot:
- Track the ball
- Help maintain pace

On the green:
- Do not stand behind putting line
- Discuss from the side only
- Use simple language

Common violations:
- Standing behind player (alignment)
- Over-coaching
- Making decisions for player
- Slow play
💡 Boston Eaglets Tip: One sentence advice is enough. The player must always make the final decision.
<br>
3. Spectator Rules
- No advice allowed at any time
- Do not help find balls unless permitted
- Stay behind players at a distance
- Stay quiet during shots
- Phones must be silent
<br>
💡 Boston Eaglets Tip: Spectator advice is one of the fastest ways to get penalized.
4. Communication Guide
Good examples:
- '7 iron, center'
- 'Safe right'
- 'Slight left break'

Avoid:
- Long explanations
- Over-analysis

<br>
5. Common Violations & Penalties
- Caddie standing behind player (alignment)
- Spectator giving advice
- Slow play
- Standing on putting line

Penalties:
- Typically +2 strokes
- Severe cases: disqualification (DQ)
<br>
6. Visual Positioning Guide
Correct: Stand to the side or behind (not directly behind the player).
Incorrect: Standing directly behind player to align.
On green: Never stand on putting line.
Spectators: Stay far back and silent.
<br>
7. Core Principles
Player: makes all decisions
Caddie: supports but does not control
Spectator: watches but does not participate

Goal: No violations + good pace + no interference
Enjoy the experience, trust yourself, and remember that every tournament is a step forward in your journey.
</p>